*Context: *Longtime readers will know that one of my pet peeves is blanket
statements about how all Chinese people embrace AI. From *ChinAI #144
<https://substack.com/redirect/960aa11b-5542-42c2-b6b6-bc9f90382658?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*,
here=E2=80=99s an emblematic quote by Bob Work, former Deputy Secretary of =
Defense
and Vice Chair of the National Security Commission on Artificial
Intelligence:

*If you go to China and talk about AI, and I haven't been to China myself,
but everyone who goes talks about the optimism Chinese citizens have about
an AI-enabled future. It=E2=80=99s not necessarily the case in the United S=
tates..I
think we've been conditioned by our movies and our TVs and our
books...[leading to] a more skeptical and possibly fearful view of an
AI-enabled future.*

I=E2=80=99ve also beat the drum on how public opinion polls that show an AI
enthusiasm gap do not make rigorous comparisons (e.g., *ChinAI #335
<https://substack.com/redirect/de3654a1-43ad-42a6-b3da-8cad611b914c?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*).


These essentializing claims then get regurgitated by =E2=80=9CChina-watcher=
s=E2=80=9D, news
articles, reports, and policymakers. Reflecting on why these statements
annoy me so much, it is not so much about a scientific standard that I hold
dear. Sure, debating the truth value of these claims matters, but I think
the root of my frustration is the *curiosity deficiency *of the claimants.

As that quote above reveals, Work (and many of his fellow regurgitators)
have never been to China. But more importantly, they are not curious about
the types of TV shows and books that draw the interest of Chinese people.

I=E2=80=99ve now been researching China=E2=80=99s AI development for the pa=
st eight years,
and sometimes, during rare flashes of self-awareness, I notice that my
thinking is growing more and more calcified. As I become more married to my
own arguments over time, it becomes more difficult to see things from a
different perspective. I think it=E2=80=99s fair to say that this calcifica=
tion
characterizes a lot of the China tech coverage we all consume.

What to do? At the end of the day, the cure is curiosity: an appetite to
learn something new. In that spirit, this week=E2=80=99s translation (link =
to *original
Chinese
<https://substack.com/redirect/f7c8bcdf-52f1-44ce-84ef-02b2b174e905?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*)
comes from a WeChat public account I discovered for the first time last
month: The Mirror [=E9=95=9C=E7=9B=B8=E5=B7=A5=E4=BD=9C=E5=AE=A4], an influ=
ential platform that covers AI through a
more critical lens. It contains references to a lot of Chinese online
communities, prose essays, and content platforms which I had never learned
about before digesting the article. I hope you discover something new too.

*Key Passages: *Yiqiu Han felt heartbroken. Her favorite author had just
posted a new novel on Tomato Novel (China=E2=80=99s top free web novel plat=
form),
but as she raced through the first ten chapters, something seemed off: =E2=
=80=9C*Why
were there so many common AI-generated phrases like =E2=80=98casting a blin=
dingly
cold light=E2=80=99? Why was the writing logic as disjointed as AI=E2=80=99=
s? Why was there
such a strange simile =E2=80=94 =E2=80=98like the sound of feeding paper th=
rough an
electrocardiogram machine=E2=80=99 =E2=80=94 in this text that was unrelate=
d to medical
settings? **Devastated, Han posted on Xiaohongshu under the hashtag #=E5=8F=
=8Dai
(#Anti-AI). As of January 2026, that hashtag had garnered 5.1 million views
and 40,000 discussion threads.*
<https://substack.com/redirect/d231e914-3192-41c4-86a2-8514136389c2?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>
Source:
*The Mirror*, screenshot of social media posts tagged #antiAI

   -

   Han=E2=80=99s story continues: =E2=80=9C*After confirming that the novel=
 was
   AI-generated, Han immediately stopped reading, unfollowed and blocked th=
e
   author she once liked, and reported it to the platform with supporting
   evidence, but has never received a response. She also deleted the push
   posts she had written for the author...*=E2=80=9D
   -

   Tomato Novel and Jinjiang Literature City (a literary platform known for
   fiction that features *Heated Rivalry-*like content) have both struggled
   to handle the influx of AI-generated content. Through March 1, 2024, ove=
r
   5,600 new books were launched on Tomato Novel; across the same period in
   2023, just 400 were launched.

The article introduces us to the struggles of audiobook narrators: =E2=80=
=9C*Two or
three years ago, Huiling Wang was still a voice actress for an audiobook
website. As someone who was totally blind but was passionate about
performing, this was a very satisfying job for her. Sitting at her desk,
listening to the incredibly fast-paced screen reader software, and using
the voice techniques she had learned in voice acting training courses, she
could record over 10,000 characters every day, producing nearly two hours
of audio output, earning two to three hundred RMB a day.*=E2=80=9D

   -

   Then, the Chinese podcast platform Ximalaya introduced AI narrators that
   could clone human voices. Per *The Mirror*, by April 2025, the
   AI-generated content rate on the Ximalaya had reached 30%.
   -

   After seeing her pay cut in half, Wang=E2=80=99s method of resistance wa=
s to
   flee. She changed jobs to work at a disability employment center: =E2=80=
=9C*At
   the time, I felt this profession wouldn=E2=80=99t be attacked by AI*.=E2=
=80=9D

*Other authors have to fight against wrongful accusations of AI-generated
writing. *Last year, Xinyi Xie became a contracted author for a web
literature platform, but she has struggled to prove the authenticity of her
writing amidst some accusations that she relied on AI.

   -

   From the article: =E2=80=9C*Xie once tried submitting her favorite Shi
   Tiesheng=E2=80=99s =E2=80=98The Temple of Earth and I=E2=80=99 to an AI =
detection website, only to
   find an AI detection rate as high as 95%, which she found absurd*.=E2=80=
=9D This
   is a famous prose essay often featured in Chinese textbooks (the Four to
   Forward section includes a link to an English translation.
   -

   Xie turned to other tactics, such as the use of inverted sentences and
   the avoidance of phrases like =E2=80=9Clight and shadow=E2=80=9D and =E2=
=80=9Cslicing,=E2=80=9D which
   people see as identifiers of AI flavor.

*Despite her 12 years of drawing experience and degree from a vocational
school with a concentration in animation design, Xiaohan struggled to find
a job: *=E2=80=9C*She checked job postings and found that almost every illu=
strator
position now included the requirement of =E2=80=98proficiency in mainstream=
 AI
software=E2=80=99*.=E2=80=9D

   -

   Her other illustrator friends would joke with each other: =E2=80=9CAfter=
 this,
   we'll only be able to set up street stalls and sell fried rice noodles.=
=E2=80=9D
   Facing financial difficulties compounded by a bipolar disorder diagnosis=
,
   Xiaohan was in a dark place.
   -

   Xiaohan returned to a character she had designed as a kid and launched a
   comic on an overseas platform, which became a hit. Riding this unexpecte=
d
   success: =E2=80=9C*She updates her comics two or three times a month, po=
sting
   three to four illustrations each time, and has designed a total of five
   characters. Through donations from her comics and commissioned work, she
   earns six to seven thousand RMB a month*.=E2=80=9D
   -

   She peppers her stories with references from her favorite Korean drama
   plots and psychological knowledge gained from her lived experience with =
her
   illness. The article concludes, =E2=80=9C*She analyzes the characters=E2=
=80=99
   motivations and audience preferences, creating characters that AI cannot
   draw and telling stories that AI cannot create, using her humanity to re=
ach
   the depths of emotion that AI can=E2=80=99t access*.=E2=80=9D

