*Thanks to my roommate for telling me I should read Shi Tiesheng. I
remember excerpts from his writing being included often in our Chinese
textbooks, and I am so unspeakably grateful that she reminded me to come
back to him as an adult.*

*Shi Tiesheng was a =E2=80=98sent-down youth=E2=80=99 from Beijing, who, du=
ring his sojourn
in the countryside, lost the use of both of his legs in a work accident.
This essay is his most famous. In it, he comes to terms with life and death
through spending time in Beijing=E2=80=99s Temple of Earth Park. The Temple=
 of
Earth is not nearly as deserted today as Shi describes, but it is still a
=E2=80=98haven of quiet=E2=80=99 compared to its flashier and more well-kno=
wn counterpart,
the Temple of Heaven. I have happy memories there and only wish I had read
this when I was still near it and able to go to it.*

