rel=3D"" style=3D"color:rgb(54,55,55);text-decoration:underline" target=3D"=
_blank">The People who Resist AI</a></strong></h2><p style=3D"margin:0 0 20=
px 0;color:rgb(54,55,55);line-height:26px;font-size:16px"><strong>Context: =
</strong><span>Longtime readers will know that one of my pet peeves is blan=
ket statements about how all Chinese people embrace AI. From </span><strong=
><a href=3D"https://substack.com/redirect/960aa11b-5542-42c2-b6b6-bc9f90382=
658?j=3DeyJ1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E" rel=
=3D"" style=3D"color:rgb(54,55,55);text-decoration:underline" target=3D"_bl=
ank">ChinAI #144</a></strong><span>, here=E2=80=99s an emblematic quote by =
Bob Work, former Deputy Secretary of Defense and Vice Chair of the National=
 Security Commission on Artificial Intelligence: </span></p><blockquote sty=
le=3D"border-left:4px solid #4dd0e1;margin:20px 0;padding:0"><p style=3D"ma=
rgin:0 0 20px 0;color:rgb(54,55,55);margin-left:20px;line-height:26px;font-=
size:16px"><em><strong>If you go to China and talk about AI, and I haven&#3=
9;t been to China myself, but everyone who goes talks about the optimism Ch=
inese citizens have about an AI-enabled future</strong><span>. It=E2=80=99s=
 not necessarily the case in the United States..I think we&#39;ve been cond=
itioned by our movies and our TVs and our books...[leading to] a more skept=
ical and possibly fearful view of an AI-enabled future.</span></em></p></bl=
ockquote><p style=3D"margin:0 0 20px 0;color:rgb(54,55,55);line-height:26px=
;font-size:16px"><span>I=E2=80=99ve also beat the drum on how public opinio=
n polls that show an AI enthusiasm gap do not make rigorous comparisons (e.=
g., </span><strong><a href=3D"https://substack.com/redirect/de3654a1-43ad-4=
2a6-b3da-8cad611b914c?j=3DeyJ1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoOD=
TUJm_FAk3tf6E" rel=3D"" style=3D"color:rgb(54,55,55);text-decoration:underl=
ine" target=3D"_blank">ChinAI #335</a></strong><span>). </span></p><p style=
=3D"margin:0 0 20px 0;color:rgb(54,55,55);line-height:26px;font-size:16px">=
<span>These essentializing claims then get regurgitated by =E2=80=9CChina-w=
atchers=E2=80=9D, news articles, reports, and policymakers. Reflecting on w=
hy these statements annoy me so much, it is not so much about a scientific =
standard that I hold dear. Sure, debating the truth value of these claims m=
atters, but I think the root of my frustration is the </span><em>curiosity =
deficiency </em><span>of the claimants.</span></p><p style=3D"margin:0 0 20=
px 0;color:rgb(54,55,55);line-height:26px;font-size:16px">As that quote abo=
ve reveals, Work (and many of his fellow regurgitators) have never been to =
China. But more importantly, they are not curious about the types of TV sho=
ws and books that draw the interest of Chinese people.</p><p style=3D"margi=
n:0 0 20px 0;color:rgb(54,55,55);line-height:26px;font-size:16px">I=E2=80=
=99ve now been researching China=E2=80=99s AI development for the past eigh=
t years, and sometimes, during rare flashes of self-awareness, I notice tha=
t my thinking is growing more and more calcified. As I become more married =
to my own arguments over time, it becomes more difficult to see things from=
 a different perspective. I think it=E2=80=99s fair to say that this calcif=
ication characterizes a lot of the China tech coverage we all consume. </p>=
<p style=3D"margin:0 0 20px 0;color:rgb(54,55,55);line-height:26px;font-siz=
e:16px"><span>What to do? At the end of the day, the cure is curiosity: an =
appetite to learn something new. In that spirit, this week=E2=80=99s transl=
ation (link to </span><strong><a href=3D"https://substack.com/redirect/f7c8=
bcdf-52f1-44ce-84ef-02b2b174e905?j=3DeyJ1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzS=
rs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E" rel=3D"" style=3D"color:rgb(54,55,55);text-decora=
tion:underline" target=3D"_blank">original Chinese</a></strong><span>) come=
s from a WeChat public account I discovered for the first time last month: =
The Mirror [=E9=95=9C=E7=9B=B8=E5=B7=A5=E4=BD=9C=E5=AE=A4], an influential =
platform that covers AI through a more critical lens. It contains reference=
s to a lot of Chinese online communities, prose essays, and content platfor=
ms which I had never learned about before digesting the article. I hope you=
 discover something new too.</span></p><p style=3D"margin:0 0 20px 0;color:=
rgb(54,55,55);line-height:26px;font-size:16px"><strong>Key Passages: </stro=
ng><span>Yiqiu Han felt heartbroken. Her favorite author had just posted a =
new novel on Tomato Novel (China=E2=80=99s top free web novel platform), bu=
t as she raced through the first ten chapters, something seemed off: =E2=80=
=9C</span><em>Why were there so many common AI-generated phrases like =E2=
=80=98casting a blindingly cold light=E2=80=99? Why was the writing logic a=
s disjointed as AI=E2=80=99s? Why was there such a strange simile =E2=80=94=
 =E2=80=98like the sound of feeding paper through an electrocardiogram mach=
ine=E2=80=99 =E2=80=94 in this text that was unrelated to medical settings?=
 </em><strong>Devastated, Han posted on Xiaohongshu under the hashtag #=E5=
